# Chroma library, modern Earth: TITLE MOMENTS of partners, family and community (titles volume 2), an everyday pair per title that only its holder meets (holds:), and one life event that gives the caregiving partner title.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md. Brief: drafts/titles2/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md. Seeds: Emren's "Modern Earth: 100 More Titles" (each pair's first moment grows from the title's turning point).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- caregiving partner (T2-047): calls BG (cycle s1) / calls WUR (cycle s2)

== the help that now feels like hovering
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
calls: BG
tone: mixed
life: love, body
horizon: week
roles: partner, a nurse, friend
holds: caregiving partner
earth: your partner, ill for months, says your help has started to feel like hovering
times: Most partners caring for an ill spouse, about once a year over the years of care; about 1 in 5 US adults give unpaid care (AARP 2020)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: Last month {partner} was glad of every hand on the stairs. This morning {partner} pushes {Ns} arm away at the bathroom door and says, quietly, that {N} never lets go any more. Love has to include another conversation.
scene W: {N} promised to see {partner} through this illness, and has kept every appointment and every pill time. Now the promise seems to need a different shape.
scene U: {N} keeps a chart of the pills, the meals and the bad days. Nowhere on it is there a column for what {partner} still wants to do alone.
scene B: {partner} has lost a job, a car and half a body's strength this year. {N} sees that the last thing {partner} owns is the say over small things, and that {N} has been taking it.
scene R: {N} stands outside the bathroom door, stung and furious and ashamed all at once. All that care, and this is the thanks.
scene G: The illness has changed the house, the days and the two of them. {N} senses that what is between them has to grow a new way round it, like a tree round a fence.
- W | sit down together and agree, out loud, what you do and what they do | v: benevolence, security | chance: 80
- U | ask the nurse which tasks they can safely do alone, and make a list | v: self-direction, security | chance: 88
- B | step back from the small jobs, and take your own evenings out again | habit | v: self-direction | chance: 90
- R | tell them straight that it hurt, and that you are tired too | v: self-direction (honesty) | act: say straight out that it hurt, and admit to being tired too | chance: 78
- G | let them take twenty minutes over the buttons, and do not reach in | v: benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 92
- W>U | keep a shared notebook where they write each day what help they want | habit | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 82
- U>B | fit a grab rail and a jar opener, so they manage alone | v: self-direction | chance: 70
- B>R | hire a carer two mornings a week, and spend them on something of your own | v: hedonism, self-direction | tags: pay:money-.05 | chance: 80
- R>G | take them out on a whim, the way you used to, wheelchair and all | body: light | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 75
- G>W | ask their sister and old friends to share the care, as families once did | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 45
works: {partner} does the buttons alone, slowly, and comes down to breakfast smiling.
works: That evening they talk for an hour, and by the next week the help fits better.
fails: {partner} goes quiet for days, and the house starts to feel like a ward.
fails: The new arrangement lasts two days before {N} is hovering again.

== a week off from caring
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
calls: WUR
tone: mixed
life: love, rest
horizon: week
roles: partner, sibling, friend
holds: caregiving partner
earth: your brother or sister offers to take over your partner's care for a week, so you can get away
times: Some caring partners, once every few years; carers' surveys find many go years without a real break (Carers UK)
gap: 2-4
scene: {sibling} rings with an offer: a whole week of looking after {partner}, so {N} can go away and sleep. The suitcase is in the loft, and {N} has not seen it in two years.
scene W: {N} made a promise, in sickness and in health. A week away feels like breaking it, even with {sibling} on duty.
scene U: {N} works out what a week would need: the pill times written down, the nurse's number, the night routine, and whether {sibling} can lift.
scene B: {N} has given two years to this, and has earned a week. A carer who breaks is no use to anyone, least of all to {partner}.
scene R: The thought of the sea, a long walk and a whole night's sleep hits {N} like a wave. {N} wants it so much it hurts.
scene G: {partner} is settled in the old routines. {N} wonders whether a week apart will rest them both, or unsettle what holds.
- W | thank them, and say your place is at home | identity | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 90
- U | write out the care hour by hour for them, then go | v: security, benevolence | chance: 65
- B | go, and ring home once a day, no more | v: self-direction, hedonism | self_control: + | chance: 80
- R | catch the first cheap train to the coast that same afternoon | door | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 92
- G | stay at home, but sleep and garden while they do the care | v: hedonism, security | chance: 78
- W>B | ask the council for a carer's assessment, and claim the respite you are owed | door | v: security | chance: 25
- U>R | plan a week of every walk and gallery you have missed | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 72
- B>G | rent a cottage by the sea for both of you, with a hired carer | v: benevolence, hedonism | tags: pay:money-.08 | chance: 45
- R>W | insist, loudly, that the whole family take turns from now on | v: universalism | chance: 45
- G>U | go to a carers' group at the library, and hear how others manage | door | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 80
works: {N} comes home after the week rested, and {partner} is glad of it.
works: The week goes smoothly, and the care at home feels lighter for a month after.
fails: {sibling} rings on the second day in a panic, and the week ends early.
fails: {N} spends the whole week worrying, and comes back more tired than before.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- nonromantic life partner (T2-048): calls WB (cycle s3) / calls URG (cycle s4)

== a form with no box for the two of you
tier: everyday
at: hospital
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-100
calls: WB
tone: mixed
life: home, love
horizon: moment
roles: partner, a nurse, friend
holds: nonromantic life partner
earth: a hospital form asks whether your life partner is your spouse, a relative or a friend
times: Most life partners outside marriage or romance, every few years at a hospital, bank or landlord (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: {partner} is waiting to go down for a small operation, and the admission form asks for next of kin: spouse, relative or friend. Twelve years of a shared home, shared bills and shared plans, and none of the boxes quite fits.
scene W: {N} wants to fill in the form truthfully. But the truth is a life two people chose on purpose, and the form has no word for it.
scene U: {N} reads the small print: a friend cannot be given test results, a spouse can. The difference is one tick.
scene B: A tick in the wrong box and {N} will be left in the corridor when the surgeon comes out. {N} has no intention of being left in any corridor.
scene R: {N} stares at the three little boxes and wants to laugh and throw the pen at once. Twelve years, and the choice is "friend".
scene G: {N} and {partner} have been family to each other for twelve years, the way some people simply are. The form was written for other lives.
- W | tick friend, then explain calmly to the nurse what you are to each other | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 82
- U | look up the hospital's rules on who may be told, and quote them | v: self-direction, security | chance: 75
- B | tick spouse, and let nobody ask | v: power, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: a false statement on a hospital form; backfire: found out at the bedside, and shut out | chance: 80
- R | cross out all three boxes and write "life partner" in capitals | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 50
- G | name their mother as next of kin, as family, and sit beside her | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 72
- W>R | ask the charge nurse, politely and properly, whether you may go down with them | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- U>G | agree together who decides what, if either of you falls ill | v: security, benevolence | chance: 88
- B>W | pay a solicitor to draw up powers of attorney for each of you | binds | v: security | tags: pay:money-.05 | chance: 70
- R>U | post a photo of the form online and ask how others manage | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | tech: internet | chance: 78
- G>B | say you are family, plainly, and stay put by the bed | v: benevolence, power | chance: 50
works: The nurse nods, writes {N} in as the one to call, and nobody asks again.
works: {N} is there when {partner} wakes, and that is all either of them wanted.
fails: {N} spends the afternoon in the corridor while the surgeon talks to someone else.
fails: The form comes back with a question mark beside {Ns} name, and the afternoon goes on phone calls.

== your life partner falls for someone
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-100
calls: URG
tone: mixed
life: love, home
horizon: months
roles: partner, friend, a new love
holds: nonromantic life partner
earth: your life partner, with whom you share a home but not a romance, falls in love with someone
times: Perhaps half of nonromantic life partnerships, once or twice over the years together (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: {partner} comes home late, sits on the arm of the sofa and says it: there is someone, and it might be serious. The house they bought together goes very quiet.
scene W: {N} and {partner} wrote down the terms of this life together years ago. Nobody wrote a line about this, and the agreement suddenly needs one.
scene U: {N} turns it over carefully: what changes, what does not, and whether a third person fits the shape of two lives built side by side.
scene B: Half the house is {Ns}, and so is half the say. {N} means to make sure this new love does not quietly take over the spare room, the evenings and the plans.
scene R: {N} is glad for {partner}, truly, and also jealous in a way that makes no sense, and cannot tell which feeling to trust.
scene G: Bonds change their shape over a life, as families do when someone marries in. {N} wonders whether this one can stretch without breaking.
- W | hold them to the agreement: no overnight guests, whatever they feel | v: conformity, security | closed: approval: policing a partner's love life; backfire: they start staying out instead, and the house goes cold | chance: 35
- U | look the new love up online, every post going back years | v: security | self_control: - | tech: internet | chance: 40
- B | make it clear the house stays as it is, whoever they date | v: power, security | chance: 40
- R | tell them you are happy for them, and jealous, and mean both | v: self-direction (honesty) | act: say, honestly, that they are glad for their partner, and jealous, and mean both | chance: 88
- G | invite the new love to Sunday dinner, and make room at the table | door | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 68
- W>G | promise each other that the bond between you comes first, whatever happens | binds | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- U>W | talk through what each of you wants, and write down fair new rules | binds | v: universalism, security | chance: 70
- B>U | start building a life of your own outside the house: a course, new friends | door | v: self-direction | mark: made a friend | chance: 88
- R>B | go out and start dating again yourself | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 62
- G>R | let it unfold, and leave them free to love whom they love | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 88
works: By spring there are three at the table some Sundays, and the house still feels like home.
works: {N} and {partner} talk it through, and what they built holds.
fails: Evenings go cold and polite, and {N} starts to wonder whose house it is.
fails: The new love is everywhere at once, and {N} feels like the lodger.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- parent of independent adult children (T2-052): calls UG (cycle s1) / calls WBR (cycle s2)

== advice on the tip of the tongue
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: mature elder
age: 42-100
calls: UG
tone: mixed
life: family
horizon: moment
roles: child, partner
holds: parent of independent adult children
earth: over coffee, your grown child mentions leaving a steady job to retrain as a chef
times: Most parents of grown children, every year or few; big turns like a new job or a move come often in children's twenties and thirties (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: Over Sunday coffee, {child} mentions, almost in passing, leaving a steady job to retrain as a chef. The old advice is ready on {Ns} tongue. This time {N} wonders whether advice is wanted at all.
scene W: {N} raised {child} to think things through and to finish what was started. Surely a parent still has a duty to say so.
scene U: {N} notices the careful, half-defensive tone in {Ns} grown child's voice, and sees that this is a new kind of conversation, adult to adult, still to be learned.
scene B: {N} knows exactly what a kitchen pays, and the bank does not care about passion. A word now could save {child} years.
scene R: The worry flares up in {Ns} chest, hot and old. And under it, something like pride: {child} has nerve.
scene G: {child} is grown, the way a tree is grown: still rooted in the same ground, but long past needing a stake. {N} lets the coffee go cold.
- W | say your piece once, clearly, and never raise it again | v: conformity, benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 68
- U | ask whether they want advice, or just someone to listen | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 95
- B | offer to help with money if they save for a year first | v: power, security | chance: 55
- R | tell them it is a mad idea, and that you love them anyway | v: self-direction (honesty), benevolence | act: call it a mad idea, and add that nothing could change how much they love their grown child | chance: 70
- G | bite your tongue, listen, and trust the way you raised them | v: tradition, benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 85
- W>U | help them set out the pros and cons on paper, the old careful way | v: self-direction | chance: 72
- U>B | look up what good chefs earn and where the best kitchens train | v: achievement | chance: 65
- B>R | quietly pay for a trial week at a cookery school | v: stimulation, benevolence | tags: pay:money-.03 | chance: 88
- R>G | cook together that evening, and once a week after, with them in charge | habit | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 92
- G>W | let it be, but ask them to keep Sunday lunch whatever happens | v: tradition | chance: 72
works: {child} talks for an hour and leaves lighter, and rings the next week to say how it went.
works: {child} leaves grateful, and the decision, whatever it is, stays {child}'s own.
fails: {child} goes quiet and changes the subject, and the next visit is a long time coming.
fails: The talk turns into the old argument, word for word, from twenty years ago.

== a grown child asks for a loan
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: mature elder
age: 42-100
calls: WBR
tone: mixed
life: family, money
horizon: months
roles: child, partner, sibling
holds: parent of independent adult children
earth: your grown child asks to borrow money for the deposit on a flat
times: Many parents of grown children, once or twice in a life for a big sum; 59 in 100 parents of young adults helped them with money in the past year (Pew 2024)
gap: 5-10
scene: {child} brings it up while drying the dishes, eyes on the plate: a deposit for a flat, a lot of money, paid back over time. {N} already knows what is in the savings account.
scene W: {N} has always treated the children the same. If {child} gets a loan, the others will have to be offered the same.
scene U: {N} wants the numbers: the price of the flat, the mortgage, {Ns} own pension, and what happens if it all goes wrong.
scene B: Money between family is power, whichever way it flows. {N} worked hard for those savings and means to lend them on terms, if at all.
scene R: {N} wants to say yes before {child} has finished the sentence. What is money for, if not for this?
scene G: {Ns} own parents helped with the first home, long ago. It is how it is done in this family, one generation lifting the next.
- W | lend it only if the other children can have the same | v: universalism, conformity | closed: means: the savings will not stretch to all of them; backfire: the pension pot runs low | chance: 45
- U | go through their budget with them before deciding anything | v: security, self-direction | chance: 75
- B | say no, plainly: the savings are for your own old age | v: security, power | chance: 85
- R | say yes on the spot, and send the money that evening | binds | v: benevolence | tags: pay:money-.1 | chance: 78
- G | give it as an early inheritance, the way your own parents did | v: tradition, benevolence | tags: pay:money-.1 | chance: 70
- W>B | lend it with a written, witnessed agreement, so nobody can argue later | v: security, power | chance: 90
- U>R | find them a cheaper flat and a better mortgage, so they can move this year | v: self-direction | chance: 25
- B>G | buy the flat yourself, and rent it to them cheaply | binds | v: security, benevolence | tags: pay:money-.15 | chance: 45
- R>W | call a family meeting and say, plainly, how the money should be shared | v: universalism | chance: 40
- G>U | tell them how you saved for your first home, and let them think | v: tradition, self-direction | act: tell their grown child how the first family home was saved for, and let them think | chance: 55
works: It is settled without a row, and the family is easy about it when the holidays come.
works: {child} gets the keys in the spring, and the money never sours a thing between them.
fails: The money sits between {N} and {child} like a stone for months.
fails: One of the other children hears about it and is hurt, and the holidays are tense.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- grandparent raising a grandchild (T2-056): calls BR (cycle s3) / calls WUG (cycle s4)

== the school gate a generation on
tier: everyday
at: school
rate: 0.5
stages: mature elder
age: 40-90
calls: BR
tone: mixed
life: children, community
horizon: week
roles: grandchild, a teacher, a young parent
holds: grandparent raising a grandchild
earth: you collect the grandchild you are raising from school, among parents half your age
times: Most grandparents raising a grandchild, each new school year; over 2 million US grandparents are raising grandchildren (Census ACS)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The school gate is familiar; {N} stood here years ago waiting for {child}. But the other parents at three o'clock belong to {Ns} children's generation, talking about apps and nap times, and they do not quite know where to put {N}.
scene W: {N} knows how a school gate works: be on time, say hello, sign up for the rota, do things properly. That much has not changed.
scene U: {N} listens to the parents' chatter like a new language: the group chats, the after-school clubs, words for things that had no words before.
scene B: {N} has more experience than everyone at this gate put together, and a grandchild whose future needs every advantage. Standing at the back will not get it.
scene R: {N} feels old and out of place, and then the grandchild comes running out waving a painting, and {N} does not care who is watching.
scene G: The gate, the bell, the children spilling out: the same rhythm, one generation on. {N} has simply come round again.
- W | put your name down for the reading rota, like any other parent | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 88
- U | ask a parent to add you to the class group chat, and learn its ways | door | v: self-direction | act: ask a parent to add them to the class group chat, and learn its ways | tech: phone | chance: 75
- B | introduce yourself to the head teacher, and make sure the grandchild is noticed | v: achievement, power | chance: 92
- R | run the parents' race at sports day, knees and all | body: light | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 85
- G | keep to your spot by the wall, and let them come to you in time | v: tradition, security | act: keep to their spot by the wall, and let the parents come to them in time | chance: 45
- W>R | throw the grandchild a proper birthday party, and invite the whole class | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 60
- U>G | read up on the games and crazes in the playground, so the grandchild fits in | v: benevolence | chance: 50
- B>W | get yourself elected to the parents' committee | identity | v: power, achievement | title: parent-association organiser | chance: 60
- R>U | admit, laughing, that you are lost, and ask a young parent to explain | v: self-direction | mark: made a friend | chance: 92
- G>B | lean on old neighbours for lifts and favours, so you can keep up | v: security | chance: 78
works: By half-term {N} is on first-name terms with three parents, and the grandchild is asked to every party.
works: A young father saves {N} a place by the gate, and the waiting goes quickly.
fails: The parents' chat goes on around {N}, as if {N} were a lamppost.
fails: The grandchild is left off a party list, and {N} wonders whether it is because of {N}.

== a call from the grandchild's parent
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: mature elder
age: 40-90
calls: WUG
tone: mixed
life: family, children
horizon: months
roles: child, grandchild, a social worker
holds: grandparent raising a grandchild
earth: your own grown child, who cannot care for their child just now, asks to have them for a weekend
times: Most grandparents raising a grandchild, every few years, as the parent's contact comes and goes (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: {child} rings on a Tuesday evening, sounding steadier than in years, and asks to have the grandchild for a whole weekend. The grandchild is doing homework at the kitchen table, within earshot.
scene W: There is an arrangement, agreed with the social worker and written down. {N} wants to honour it, and to be fair to {child}.
scene U: {N} thinks it through: how {child} has been these last months, what the grandchild has said, what a weekend could start and what it could undo.
scene B: {N} has fought to give the grandchild a steady life. Whatever {child} wants, the grandchild's future comes first, and {N} means to decide what that requires.
scene R: {Ns} heart leaps and sinks at once. {child} sounds well. But {N} remembers every other time {child} sounded well.
scene G: Children belong with their parents when they can, and {child} is still the grandchild's parent. {N} feels the family trying to mend itself.
- W | say yes only if the social worker agrees, as the arrangement says | v: conformity, security | chance: 75
- U | suggest an afternoon first, and see how it goes | v: security | chance: 70
- B | say no: the grandchild's settled life comes first, for now | v: security, power | chance: 80
- R | say yes, and drive the grandchild over yourself on Friday | v: benevolence | tech: car | chance: 62
- G | invite them to spend the weekend in your house, the family together | v: tradition, benevolence | closed: approval: going round the social worker's arrangement; backfire: the social worker hears of it, and the arrangement is reviewed | chance: 65
- W>G | write down a family agreement for visits, so everyone knows where they stand | v: security, tradition | chance: 55
- U>W | ask the social worker what the arrangement allows, before answering | v: conformity, security | chance: 88
- B>U | agree, on condition they keep seeing their counsellor first | v: power, security | chance: 42
- R>B | tell them, hotly, that they will have to earn it this time | v: power, security | mark: made an enemy | chance: 35
- G>R | take everyone to the park together, and let it be easy for one afternoon | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 90
works: The grandchild comes home chattering, and {child} rings again the next week, still steady.
works: Whatever is decided, it is decided calmly, and the family starts, slowly, to feel like one again.
fails: {child} does not turn up, and {N} has to find the words for the grandchild.
fails: There is a row on the phone, and the arrangement grows stiffer for months.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- choir member (T2-059): calls WG (cycle s1) / calls UBR (cycle s2)

== the new voice that rushes the phrase
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 12-100
calls: WG
tone: mixed
life: community, music
horizon: week
roles: a choir director, friend, a new singer
holds: choir member
earth: the choir's newest singer has a beautiful voice, and keeps rushing ahead of everyone
times: Most choir members, every few years as new singers join; about 1 in 6 Americans sing in a choir (Chorus America 2019)
gap: 2-4
scene: The newest member has a beautiful voice, and keeps rushing the long phrase in the second verse, half a beat ahead of everyone. The director sighs, and the whole row feels it. The choir has to make room without losing each other.
scene W: The choir breathes together at the end of the phrase; that is the point of a choir. {N} wants the newcomer to learn it, the way {N} once had to.
scene U: {N} listens closely and hears why: the newcomer breathes in the wrong place, runs short of air, and hurries to the end.
scene B: That voice could carry the spring concert, and {N} knows it. Whoever helps the newcomer settle will have an ally in the front row.
scene R: Rushing or not, the newcomer sounds thrilling. {N} would rather the choir caught some of that fire than snuffed it out.
scene G: Every choir takes a season to take in a new voice, the way a river takes in a stream. {N} has seen it happen before.
- W | ask the director to take the phrase slowly, section by section, for everyone | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 85
- U | show the newcomer where to breathe, quietly, at the tea break | v: benevolence, self-direction | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- B | befriend the newcomer before anyone else does, and keep them close | door | v: power, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 90
- R | sing out boldly beside them, so they hear where the beat is | v: stimulation | chance: 45
- G | stand next to them every week, and let them settle into the sound | habit | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
- W>U | set up a practice half hour before rehearsal for anyone who wants it | habit | v: achievement, benevolence | chance: 72
- U>B | record the section on your phone and show the director who drifts | v: achievement, power | closed: approval: recording singers without asking; backfire: the newcomer finds out and stops coming | tech: phone | chance: 45
- B>R | offer to coach them yourself, in return for a duet at the concert | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 65
- R>G | start every warm-up with a silly round, so everyone laughs together | habit | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 92
- G>W | tell the newcomer how the choir has always sung it, and why | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 40
works: By the third rehearsal the phrase lands together, and the room rings with it.
works: The newcomer stays, and the whole section sounds better for it.
fails: The newcomer stops coming after a month, and nobody quite says why.
fails: Rehearsal turns tense, and two singers stop speaking at the tea urn.

== a solo at the spring concert
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 12-100
calls: UBR
tone: joy
life: community, music
horizon: months
roles: a choir director, rival, friend
holds: choir member
earth: the director offers you the solo at the spring concert
times: Some choir members, a few times in a life; most choir singers rarely or never sing a solo (estimate)
gap: 5-10
scene: After rehearsal the director catches {Ns} sleeve: would {N} sing the solo at the spring concert? Sixteen bars, alone, in front of four hundred people. {rival} wanted it badly.
scene W: The solo belongs to the piece, and the piece belongs to the whole choir. {N} wonders whether saying yes is service or showing off.
scene U: {N} hears the sixteen bars on the way home: the high note in bar eleven, the breath before it, everything that has to be practised.
scene B: Four hundred people, the local paper, a voice of {Ns} own beyond the part {N} plays everywhere else. This is a chance, and {N} means to take it.
scene R: {N} walks home grinning and terrified, singing the first line out loud at the bus stop.
scene G: {N} has sung in the back row for years, happily part of the whole. Standing alone at the front feels like a tree stepping out of the wood.
