scene U: {N} does the sums on a napkin: twenty-two members, three nights a month, two a head, or a library room, or a pub that wants the trade.
scene B: Forty a night is an opening offer, not a verdict. {N} knows what twenty-two thirsty players are worth to a café on a Thursday.
scene R: {N} is annoyed for a moment, then oddly excited. Perhaps this is the push the club needed to find somewhere better.
scene G: The club has its corner, its scuffed shelf, its regulars at the same tables. {N} feels the roots of a thing that has grown in one place.
- W | call a members' meeting and agree a small fee per night, fairly | v: conformity, universalism | chance: 75
- U | compare every hall, pub and library room in town before deciding | v: self-direction, security | chance: 80
- B | talk the owner down to half, in return for food orders | v: power, achievement | chance: 40
- R | move the club to the pub across the road that same week | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 50
- G | give up the organising to a keen younger member, and just play | v: benevolence, tradition | self_control: - | drops: board-game club organiser | chance: 80
- W>R | set up a proper membership fund, so the club can go wherever it likes | v: security, self-direction | chance: 45
- U>G | work out a rota for holding club nights in members' homes | v: benevolence | chance: 40
- B>W | register the club as a society, with dues, a bank account and you as chair | identity | v: achievement, power | act: register the club as a society, with dues, a bank account and themselves as chair | chance: 85
- R>U | try a different venue every week for a month, and see what works | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 70
- G>B | ask the old friend who runs the pub to give you a room for nothing | v: security | act: ask the old friend who runs the pub for a room for nothing | chance: 40
works: Within a month the club has a home again, and nobody misses a Thursday.
works: The members chip in without a murmur, and the shelf of games stays where it is.
fails: The club loses half its members in the move, and Thursdays go thin.
fails: Talks with the owner go sour, and the club spends a month with nowhere to meet.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: your partner falls seriously ill (gives the caregiving partner title; pairs D5a)
# Gate for the engine: this event needs the person to hold a partner title (held_partner), so the engine can add it to
# earth_rules.LIFE: "your partner falls seriously ill": dict(req="held_partner"). The engine does not model a partner's
# health, so this event is how a person comes to hold [caregiving partner]. The options that take up the care carry
# title: caregiving partner, each in a color that fits one of the title's profiles: Emren's White-Green (W, G, WG:
# continuing care made part of the bond and the shared life) and Black (B: the partner's agency kept, with boundaries
# around one's own capacity), and the Red third profile of the catalogue part drafts/titles2/others.py (WR: warmth and
# family occasions kept alive in a life reshaped by care).

== your partner falls seriously ill
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .008 .02 .045
drivers: health-.3 money-.3 harsh+.3 stress+.2 era-.2 trouble+.1
gap: 3-8
times: estimate, per person with a partner a year: about 0.8 in 100 adults, 2 in 100 in midlife and 4.5 in 100 elders see their partner fall seriously ill in a way that needs months or years of care (a cancer, a stroke or heart attack, dementia, a long illness or injury); it leans on D64 (cancer, 0.2, 0.7 and 2 in 100 a year), D65 (heart attack or stroke, 0.1, 0.5 and 1.5), D70 (dementia, 1 to 8 in 100 a year from 65) and D50 (about 1 family carer in 8 cares for a spouse or partner, AARP and NAC 2025), with a partner of about the same age; set per person with a partner (needs: partner); before 25 it is rare enough to leave out
likelier: older age, smoking or heavy drinking in the household, poverty, hard physical work, a harsh place to live, long stress
rarer: money for good health care, healthy habits, a younger partner, a prosperous and peaceful time
needs: partner
domain: partner1
tone: trouble
life: love, body
horizon: years
roles: partner, a doctor, child, sibling, friend
earth: your partner is diagnosed with a serious illness that will need care for months or years
scene: In a small room off the ward, the consultant draws a diagram on a pad, and {partner} squeezes {Ns} hand so hard it hurts. It is serious, it will be long, and nothing at home will be quite the same.
scene W: {N} made a promise, years ago, for better or for worse. Sitting in the consultant's room, {N} understands that this is the worse, and that the promise is now a daily one.
scene U: {N} writes down every word the consultant says: the name of the illness, the stages, the treatments, the odds. Understanding it feels like the first thing that can be done.
scene B: {N} sees the whole road at once: the appointments, the money, the job, {Ns} own strength. Somebody has to keep all of it standing, and {partner} still has to be the one who decides about {partner}'s own life.
scene R: {N} walks out into the hospital car park and shouts at the sky, then goes back in and holds {partner} as if letting go would make it happen.
scene G: Bodies fail, as they do; {N} has always known it, the way one knows winter will come. Now it has come to the house, and {N} means to meet it there, together.
- W | promise to see them through it, and rearrange your life around the care | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: kept your word | title: caregiving partner | chance: 72
- U | learn everything about the illness and the help on offer, before deciding who does what | v: self-direction, security | mark: learned a skill | chance: 78
- B | take on the care on clear terms: they decide what they can, you keep working | v: self-direction, security | title: caregiving partner | act: take on the care on clear terms: the partner decides all they still can, and the carer keeps working | chance: 72
- R | take them away now on the trip you always meant to take | v: stimulation, hedonism | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: the doctors advise against travel; backfire: a crisis far from home | tags: pay:money-.08 | chance: 62
- G | care for them at home, in your own rhythm, for as long as it takes | habit | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | title: caregiving partner | grants: home nursing | chance: 68
- BG | accept the long road, and quietly set money aside for the hard years | v: security | self_control: + | chance: 85
- UB | find the best specialist in the country, and pull every string to get them seen | v: power, security | chance: 50
- UR | push for a second opinion at once, and chase every result yourself | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 75
- WG | weave the care into your shared life, with family and friends on a rota | v: benevolence, tradition | title: caregiving partner | chance: 60
- WR | take up the care, and keep every birthday, outing and visit going, illness or not | habit | v: benevolence, tradition | self_control: + | title: caregiving partner | chance: 78
works: {partner} comes through the worst of it, and the two of them find a new way of living with what remains.
works: The months are hard, but {N} and {partner} meet them together, and come out closer.
fails: The illness takes more than anyone expected, and by spring {N} is worn thin.
fails: The care comes apart in a hard winter, and nobody can agree what should happen next.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: going into foster care (gives former foster child, T2-091;
# the engine's ask 16:28, in place of a background grant). The reason is never shown: the grown-ups at home cannot look after the child for now.

== going into foster care
tier: life event
stages: child
age: 3-12
per_year: .003 0 0 0 0 0
drivers: money-.4 stress+.4 ties-.3 trouble+.2
times: once: about 6 in 100 American children enter foster care at some point before 18 (Wildeman and Emanuel 2014), and about 1 child in 100 is in care at any time in England; set at 3 in 1,000 a year from 3 to 12, so about 3 in 100 children by 12, the catalogue's share for former foster child (estimate for rich countries, where relatives often step in first)
likelier: deep poverty, a parent's serious illness, addiction or prison, a parent alone with no help, a family in crisis
rarer: grandparents or relatives who can take the child in, enough money, a steady family, a close community
tone: trouble
life: family
horizon: years
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent
title: former foster child
earth: you go to live with a foster family for a while, because the grown-ups at home cannot look after you for now
scene: A social worker with a kind voice drives {N} across town with one bag on the back seat. The grown-ups at home cannot look after {N} for now, and a family {N} has never met is waiting with the porch light on.
scene W: {N} wants to know the rules of the new house straight away: bedtimes, which chair, what is allowed. If {N} keeps every rule, maybe nobody will have to move {N} again.
scene U: {N} counts the turns on the drive and reads every sign, trying to work out how far away home is, and how long "for now" means.
scene B: {N} learned a while ago not to count on anyone else. In a new house it is worth knowing where the food is kept and who is really in charge.
scene R: {N} presses a face to the car window. A new house means a new garden, new rooms, maybe a dog. {N} is scared, and a little curious too.
scene G: {N} holds the bag tight on the back seat. Inside is the one thing from home that matters, and whatever happens, it stays with {N}.
- W | learn the house rules on the first night, and keep every one | v: security, conformity | chance: 80
- U | ask the social worker exactly what happens next, and when you can go home | v: self-direction, security | chance: 78
- B | keep a secret store of snacks under the bed, just in case | v: security | chance: 88
- R | explore the whole new house and garden, and make friends with the dog by teatime | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 75
- G | keep the thing from home with you every night, under the pillow | v: tradition, security | act: keep the thing from home close every night, under the pillow | chance: 75
- WG | make a card for your mum or dad every week, ready for the next visit | habit | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 80
- UB | work out which grown-up really decides things, and ask them, not the others | v: power, security | act: work out which grown-up really decides things, and ask that one, not the others | chance: 60
- RG | make friends with the other foster child in the house before bedtime | v: benevolence, stimulation | mark: made a friend | chance: 70
- WB | be so helpful around the house that the family will want to keep you | v: conformity, security | act: be so helpful around the house that the family will want to keep them | chance: 60
- UR | keep a diary of everything that happens, to tell it right one day | habit | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 80
works: The foster family keeps the porch light on every night, and slowly {N} stops listening for the car that will take {N} away.
works: {N} goes home in the spring, with a box of drawings and a phone number to call if things get hard again.
fails: Three houses in a year, and {N} learns not to unpack.
fails: The visits home are cancelled twice, and {N} stops asking when the next one is.
